1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,348
Everything you've written so far is seen from a bridge.

2
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:06,987
One ship, one crew,

3
00:00:07,437 --> 00:00:09,611
one system at a time.

4
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:14,020
There's another seat in this game.

5
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:17,093
The Admiral doesn't fly anything.

6
00:00:17,993 --> 00:00:21,472
The Admiral looks down on a whole system,

7
00:00:21,922 --> 00:00:25,400
builds on its worlds, and sends fleets out.

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,809
Today you write for that seat.

9
00:00:31,009 --> 00:00:36,661
Before we start, here's how this lecture and the next one fit together.

10
00:00:38,461 --> 00:00:41,504
Your own universe can have an admiral.

11
00:00:42,404 --> 00:00:44,143
That's the next lecture.

12
00:00:45,943 --> 00:00:48,987
Today we work somewhere else, on purpose.

13
00:00:49,887 --> 00:00:53,365
The Open Universe mission holds a small universe

14
00:00:53,815 --> 00:00:56,859
that is nothing but an admiral's game.

15
00:00:57,759 --> 00:01:03,411
Three kinds of world, six officers, and no story to keep track of.

16
00:01:05,211 --> 00:01:09,559
It's the best place to learn what each line does.

17
00:01:10,459 --> 00:01:15,676
So we make a copy of it, and we change the copy.

18
00:01:17,476 --> 00:01:20,085
And what you write there today

19
00:01:20,535 --> 00:01:24,883
is exactly what goes into your own universe next time.

20
00:01:26,083 --> 00:01:28,257
First, we make a copy.

21
00:01:29,157 --> 00:01:32,200
Never change the Open Universe folder itself,

22
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:35,259
because an update will replace it.

23
00:01:37,059 --> 00:01:41,841
I copy it, paste it, and call the copy Admiral Lab.

24
00:01:43,641 --> 00:01:45,380
One warning about saves.

25
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:50,628
A save is named after the universe, not the folder,

26
00:01:51,078 --> 00:01:54,991
so the lab and the real thing share one.

27
00:01:55,891 --> 00:01:59,804
If you've got a skirmish game you care about,

28
00:02:00,254 --> 00:02:02,863
move that file somewhere safe first.

29
00:02:04,063 --> 00:02:08,846
Here's the whole game, so you know what you're writing for.

30
00:02:10,646 --> 00:02:14,559
The Admiral selects a world, and builds a headquarters.

31
00:02:15,459 --> 00:02:17,198
Then comes an extractor,

32
00:02:17,648 --> 00:02:19,822
and ore starts coming in.

33
00:02:20,722 --> 00:02:23,330
Then an academy and a shipyard,

34
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:26,824
and the shipyard offers officers to commission.

35
00:02:27,724 --> 00:02:31,202
Each officer gets a fleet of three ships.

36
00:02:32,102 --> 00:02:34,276
And a lab offers research.

37
00:02:36,076 --> 00:02:39,120
You don't write any of those buildings.

38
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:42,628
They're the same in every universe.

39
00:02:43,528 --> 00:02:47,441
You write the worlds, the people, and the ladder.

40
00:02:48,641 --> 00:02:52,120
This is the arena file, and it's short.

41
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:56,063
Here are its three kinds of world.

42
00:02:56,513 --> 00:02:59,122
I'm adding a fourth, above them.

43
00:03:00,922 --> 00:03:04,835
Yields is what one extractor brings in each minute.

44
00:03:05,735 --> 00:03:10,517
Reserve is how much there is before the world runs dry.

45
00:03:12,317 --> 00:03:14,926
And here's the rule that matters.

46
00:03:15,826 --> 00:03:18,870
The home system always gets one world,

47
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:24,537
and it's the first one in the file that never runs dry.

48
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:27,176
I put mine first,

49
00:03:27,626 --> 00:03:30,670
so my Admiral starts on my world.

50
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:36,217
Next come the dials, and there are three I'm setting.

51
00:03:37,117 --> 00:03:38,857
There's the starting ore,

52
00:03:39,307 --> 00:03:41,480
how many fleets at once,

53
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:44,974
and no border raids while I'm learning.

54
00:03:46,774 --> 00:03:48,513
Now, an honest number.

55
00:03:49,413 --> 00:03:51,587
I wrote five hundred ore.

56
00:03:52,487 --> 00:03:55,096
The game started with six thousand.

57
00:03:56,896 --> 00:04:00,809
Part of that is the mode, which is fair.

58
00:04:01,709 --> 00:04:03,448
But most of it

59
00:04:03,898 --> 00:04:08,246
is a test setting the game's makers left switched on.

60
00:04:09,146 --> 00:04:12,624
It runs the Admiral's economy eight times fast.

61
00:04:14,424 --> 00:04:16,598
So don't balance anything today.

62
00:04:17,498 --> 00:04:20,107
Write the numbers your story suggests,

63
00:04:20,557 --> 00:04:24,035
and tune them when that setting is gone.

64
00:04:25,235 --> 00:04:28,713
Officers look like the captains from Lecture Eight,

65
00:04:29,163 --> 00:04:32,207
and their values are the same traits.

66
00:04:34,007 --> 00:04:37,485
Here, though, three of those traits do something.

67
00:04:38,385 --> 00:04:42,298
By the book means the fleet burns less gas.

68
00:04:43,198 --> 00:04:44,937
Resourceful means more salvage.

69
00:04:45,837 --> 00:04:48,880
Fearsome means it engages from farther out.

70
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,854
Every other trait is character.

71
00:04:53,754 --> 00:04:55,493
It's who she is,

72
00:04:55,943 --> 00:04:59,422
for the day a crew talks to her.

73
00:05:00,622 --> 00:05:04,970
The arena has no research, so I'm adding the chapter.

74
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:07,609
There are two steps.

75
00:05:08,509 --> 00:05:12,422
Each one has a cost, a time in seconds,

76
00:05:12,872 --> 00:05:14,611
and what it unlocks.

77
00:05:15,511 --> 00:05:18,120
The second one requires the first,

78
00:05:18,570 --> 00:05:21,613
and that's what makes it a ladder.

79
00:05:23,413 --> 00:05:25,152
Now I run lint.

80
00:05:26,052 --> 00:05:29,096
Two warnings were there before I started,

81
00:05:29,546 --> 00:05:31,720
in files I never touched.

82
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:34,793
And that's all there is.

83
00:05:35,243 --> 00:05:37,417
My own file says clean.

84
00:05:39,217 --> 00:05:41,391
One small thing about time.

85
00:05:42,291 --> 00:05:44,900
It's a plain number of seconds.

86
00:05:45,350 --> 00:05:47,089
Forty means forty seconds.

87
00:05:48,889 --> 00:05:54,107
So for today, those two old warnings are what done looks like.

88
00:05:55,307 --> 00:05:57,046
Now let's play it.

89
00:05:57,946 --> 00:06:03,598
On the start screen I pick the skirmish universe, and a new game.

90
00:06:05,398 --> 00:06:08,876
There's one world at home, and it's mine.

91
00:06:09,776 --> 00:06:11,515
I build a headquarters,

92
00:06:11,965 --> 00:06:16,313
then the extractor, the academy, the shipyard and the lab.

93
00:06:17,213 --> 00:06:18,952
The ore is climbing.

94
00:06:20,752 --> 00:06:23,361
The shipyard offers my officer first,

95
00:06:23,811 --> 00:06:26,420
by her name and her title.

96
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:30,363
And the lab offers my first step,

97
00:06:30,813 --> 00:06:33,857
and then, once that's done, my second.

98
00:06:35,057 --> 00:06:36,796
Now it's your turn.

99
00:06:37,696 --> 00:06:42,478
In the lab, write the world your own side starts on,

100
00:06:42,928 --> 00:06:46,841
then two officers, and a ladder of three steps.

101
00:06:48,641 --> 00:06:51,250
Write them for your own universe,

102
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:53,874
because that's where they're going.

103
00:06:54,774 --> 00:06:58,252
It's three small moves to bring them home,

104
00:06:58,702 --> 00:07:00,876
and they're on the page.

105
00:07:02,676 --> 00:07:05,285
The next lecture makes those moves,

106
00:07:05,735 --> 00:07:09,213
and puts an admiral beside your bridge crew.
